  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'rschema_0307' contains six tables: Companies_Table, API_Keys_Table, Employees_Table, Sites_Table, Devices_Table, and Users_Table.\nThe Companies_Table represents the company details. It includes columns for Company_ID (integer, primary key), Company_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Company_Website (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Creation_Date (datetime), and Update_Date (datetime).\nThe API_Keys_Table stores API keys. It includes columns for Application_Reason (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Creation_Date (datetime), Key_ID (integer, primary key), Key_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Private_Key (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), URI (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), and Update_Date (datetime).\nThe Employees_Table contains employee details. It includes columns for Company_ID (integer), Creation_Date (datetime), Employee_ID (integer, primary key), Employee_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Position (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Salary (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), and Update_Date (datetime).\nThe Sites_Table contains site details. It includes columns for Creation_Date (datetime), Database_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Hostname (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Is_Deprecated (boolean), Site_Abbreviation (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Site_ID (integer, primary key), Site_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), and Update_Date (datetime).\nThe Devices_Table contains device details. It includes columns for Associated_Employee_ID (integer), Creation_Date (datetime), Device_ID (integer, primary key), Device_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), and Update_Date (datetime).\nFinally, the Users_Table contains user details. It includes columns for Company_ID (integer), Creation_Date (datetime), Display_Name_Usage (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Email (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Is_Admin (boolean), Is_Developer (boolean), Last_Login_Time (datetime), Password (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Password_Salt (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Password_Type (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Real_Name (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), Site_ID (integer, not null), Update_Date (datetime), User_ID (integer, primary key), Username (varchar with a maximum length of 255 characters), and a foreign key constraint on Site_ID referencing the Sites_Table.",
  "gold_question": "The requirements analysis for the company's internal management system is as follows:  \n\nCompany Management:  \nStore company information, including Company ID, Creation Date, Update Date, Company Website, and Company Name. The Company Name is unique. Operations include adding, modifying, and deleting company information, such as the company name and website.  \n\nUser Management:  \nStore user information, including User ID, Creation Date, Update Date, Is Admin, Real Name, Password Type, Password Salt, Username, Email, Password, Display Name Usage, Is Developer, and Last Login Time. The Username and Email are unique. Operations include registering new users, modifying user information, setting admin and developer roles, and recording the last login time. A company can have multiple users.  \n\nAPI Key Management:  \nStore API key information, including Key ID, Creation Date, Update Date, Private Key, Application Reason, Key Name, and URI. The Key Name is unique and is associated with the user table. A user can only have one API key. Users can apply for an API key and provide a reason for the application.  \n\nEmployee Management:  \nStore employee information, including Employee ID, Creation Date, Update Date, Employee Name, Position, and Salary. This is associated with the company table, and a company can have multiple employees. Operations include managing employee information such as name, position, and salary, as well as assigning employees to different companies.  \n\nSite Management:  \nStore site information, including Site ID, Creation Date, Update Date, Database Name, Is Deprecated, Site Name, Hostname, and Site Abbreviation. The Database Name and Site Abbreviation are unique and are associated with the user table. A user can only use one site. Operations include managing site information such as site name, hostname, and site abbreviation, as well as marking sites as deprecated.  \n\nDevice Management:  \nStore device information, including Device ID, Creation Date, Update Date, Device Name, and Associated Employee ID. This is associated with the employee table, and an employee can manage multiple devices.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0307.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0307\nCREATE TABLE `companies_table` (\n  `company_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `company_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `company_website` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `update_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`company_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `api_keys_table` (\n  `key_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `key_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `private_key` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `uri` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_reason` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `update_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`key_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `employees_table` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `company_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `employee_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `salary` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `update_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `employees_table_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`company_id`) REFERENCES `companies_table` (`company_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sites_table` (\n  `site_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `site_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `site_abbreviation` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `hostname` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `database_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_deprecated` boolean DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `update_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`site_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `devices_table` (\n  `device_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `device_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `associated_employee_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `update_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`device_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `devices_table_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`associated_employee_id`) REFERENCES `employees_table` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users_table` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `company_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `site_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name_usage` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `is_admin` boolean DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_developer` boolean DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_login_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password_salt` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `password_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `real_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `update_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `users_table_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`company_id`) REFERENCES `companies_table` (`company_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `users_table_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`site_id`) REFERENCES `sites_table` (`site_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
